[38;2;124;124;124mA uniformity on a set X induces a topology. The question is whether there exists[0m[38;2;124;124;124ma topological space (X, τ) such that there exist two uniformities U and V on X, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mboth inducing τ, where U is complete and V is incomplete.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- A topological space is uniformizable iff it is completely regular (Tychonoff, [0m[38;2;124;124;124massuming T1).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- A uniform space is complete if every Cauchy filter converges.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Completeness depends on the uniformity, not just the topology.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mClassic example: The real line ℝ with its usual topology.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- The usual metric uniformity (induced by |x-y|) is complete.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- But we can put a different uniformity inducing the same topology that is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mincomplete.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHow? Consider ℝ with the metric d(x,y) = |arctan(x) - arctan(y)|. This induces [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe same topology as the usual one (since arctan is a homeomorphism from ℝ to (-[0m[38;2;124;124;124mπ/2, π/2)). But is this uniformity complete?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA sequence like x_n = n is Cauchy in this uniformity because arctan(n) → π/2, so[0m[38;2;124;124;124m|arctan(n) - arctan(m)| → 0. But n does not converge in ℝ (in this uniformity, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconvergence is the same as topological convergence, and n doesn't converge in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe usual topology). So this uniformity is incomplete.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, let me be more careful. The metric d(x,y) = |arctan(x) - arctan(y)| on ℝ. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mThe topology induced by d: a set is open if... arctan: ℝ → (-π/2, π/2) is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mhomeomorphism (with usual topologies). The metric d is the pullback of the usual[0m[38;2;124;124;124mmetric on (-π/2, π/2) via arctan. So the topology induced by d on ℝ is the same [0m[38;2;124;124;124mas the usual topology on ℝ (since arctan is a homeomorphism).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, is (ℝ, d) complete? Take the sequence x_n = n. Then arctan(n) → π/2. So [0m
[38;2;124;124;124md(n, m) = |arctan(n) - arctan(m)| → 0 as n, m → ∞. So (x_n) is Cauchy. But does [0m[38;2;124;124;124mit converge? If x_n → x in (ℝ, d), then d(x_n, x) → 0, i.e., |arctan(n) - [0m
[38;2;124;124;124marctan(x)| → 0, so arctan(x) = π/2. But arctan(x) < π/2 for all real x. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mContradiction. So (x_n) does not converge, hence (ℝ, d) is incomplete.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAnd the usual metric uniformity on ℝ (from |x-y|) is complete.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo ℝ with its usual topology is induced by:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- The usual uniformity (complete)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- The arctan uniformity (incomplete)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore, yes, such a topology exists.[0m

